      content:
          '**Primary Diagnosis:** Critical illness myopathy (CIM) — ICU-acquired weakness in the setting of prolonged corticosteroid and neuromuscular blocking agent exposure.\n\n**Key Diagnostic Reasoning:**\n- **CIM vs CIP distinction:** CIM presents with preserved sensory nerve action potentials (SNAPs) on NCS, reduced compound muscle action potential (CMAP) amplitudes, and myopathic motor unit potentials on EMG. CIP shows reduced SNAPs AND CMAPs with neuropathic recruitment. Mixed CIM/CIP is common.\n- **EMG/NCS findings expected:** Low CMAP amplitudes with normal SNAPs, myopathic short-duration polyphasic MUPs, early recruitment, possible fibrillation potentials and positive sharp waves in proximal and distal muscles. Direct muscle stimulation showing reduced muscle-to-nerve CMAP ratio is highly specific for CIM.\n- **Medication culprits:** Synergistic toxicity of corticosteroids + neuromuscular blocking agents is the classic combination causing CIM. Steroids cause selective thick filament (myosin) loss. NMBAs prolong exposure.\n- **CK elevation pattern:** Elevated CK supports myopathic process; trending down from peak suggests resolving acute phase.\n- **Differential diagnosis:** Must exclude pre-existing myopathy, prolonged NMB effect (train-of-four testing), spinal cord pathology, Guillain-Barré syndrome, and rhabdomyolysis.\n- **Ventilator weaning failure:** Diaphragmatic myopathy from CIM is a major contributor — assess with diaphragm ultrasound or transdiaphragmatic pressure measurement.',
