          '**Q: The patient fails his 5th spontaneous breathing trial. What is your ventilator weaning strategy incorporating rehabilitation?**\n\n**A:** Implement a **rehabilitation-integrated weaning protocol**:\n1. **Assess diaphragm function:** Bedside diaphragm ultrasound measuring thickening fraction (normal >30%; <20% predicts weaning failure). Consider phrenic nerve conduction studies if diaphragm dysfunction suspected.\n2. **Inspiratory muscle training (IMT):** Once patient tolerates brief tracheostomy collar trials, use threshold IMT device starting at 30% of maximal inspiratory pressure, progressing to 50-60%. Perform 5 sets of 6 breaths, twice daily.\n3. **Progressive tracheostomy collar trials:** Start at whatever duration he tolerates (even 5 minutes), increase by 15-30 minutes per session. Use a structured sprint-and-rest protocol rather than continuous weaning.\n4. **Upright positioning during weaning trials:** Sitting position improves diaphragm mechanics vs supine.\n5. **Concurrent whole-body strengthening:** Peripheral muscle weakness correlates with diaphragm weakness — total body rehabilitation supports weaning.\n6. **Monitor closely:** Heart rate, respiratory rate, SpO2, tidal volumes, subjective dyspnea. Abort trial for HR >140, RR >35, SpO2 <88%, or patient distress.\n7. **Nutritional adequacy:** Malnourished patients cannot wean — ensure protein and caloric goals are met.\n8. **Timeline:** If no progress in 2-3 weeks with aggressive rehabilitation, consider LTAC transfer for prolonged weaning facility.',
